+ x_S b_S\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\nE[B] = x_A \\cdot 2 \\times 10^5 + x_C \\cdot 2 \\times 10^4 + x_S \\cdot 3 \\times 10^4\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression:\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 200 \\times 10^5 x_A + 200 \\times 10^5 x_C + 300 \\times 10^4 x_S = (200 \\times 10^5 + 300 \\times 10^4) x_A + 200 \\times 10^5 x_C\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 230 \\times 10^5 x_A + 200 \\times 10^5 x_C\n\\]\n\nTo maximize \\(E[B]\\), we need to find the optimal values of \\(x_A\\), \\(x_C\\), and \\(x_S\\). Since \\(x_S\\) is the smallest (0.0001), it is the most appropriate to be allocated first, followed by \\(x_C\\) and then \\(x_A\\), given that it is the most valuable. Therefore, the optimal allocation is:\n\n\\[\nx_C = 0.0001, \\quad x_S = 0, \\quad x_A = 1 - 0.0001 - 0 = 0.9999\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expected benefit expression:\n\n\\[\nE[B] = (230 \\times 10^5 + 300 \\times 10^4) \\cdot 0.9999 + 200 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = (230 \\times 10^5 + 30 \\times 10^5) \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.9999 + 2 \\times 10^5 \\cdot 0.0001\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE[B] = 260 \\times 10^5 \\